WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:11.490
Reactome has another tool called ReactomeGSA
which allows users to analyse proteomics,

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:15.190
transcriptomics and microarray data.

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:19.410
Datasets from all of these methods can be
combined in a single analysis.

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:24.930
Thereby, ReactomeGSA can perform multi-omics
pathway analyses.

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:29.949
Its main feature is that it performs quantitative
pathway analyses, the so called “gene set

00:00:29.949 --> 00:00:31.779
analyses”.

00:00:31.779 --> 00:00:36.220
This increases the statistical power of the
differential expression analysis, which is

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:39.790
directly performed on the pathway level.

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:45.860
ReactomeGSA is integrated into Reactome’s
data analysis tool suite in the Analyze Data

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:47.230
interface.

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:53.260
The input file must be a tab-delimited text
file, such as CSV or TSV file, where the first

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:58.610
column contains the gene or protein identifiers
and all subsequent columns the respective

00:00:58.610 --> 00:00:59.760
samples.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:05.780
The first row contains the sample names and
all subsequent rows the genes or proteins.

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:10.810
In the first screen, you need to select the
algorithm to use for the differential pathway

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:11.960
analysis.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:16.320
The detailed parameters for each algorithm
can be adapted by clicking the blue icon on

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:19.140
the left of the algorithm’s box.

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:24.790
PADOG and Camera perform a differential expression
analysis between two groups of samples.

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:31.140
ssGSEA is a so-called gene set variation approach
that returns pathway-level quantitative data

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:33.030
for each sample.

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:38.400
PADOG stands for Pathway Analysis with Down-weighting
of Overlapping Genes computes a gene set score

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:42.350
based on gene weights that are designed to
emphasize the genes appearing in few gene

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:46.810
sets, versus genes that appear in many gene
sets.

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:50.290
Inter-gene correlations can skew analysis
results.

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:55.330
Camera or Correlation Adjusted MEan RAnk gene
set test is based on the idea of estimating

00:01:55.330 --> 00:02:01.729
the inter-gene correlation from the data,
and using it to adjust the gene set test statistic.

00:02:01.729 --> 00:02:08.040
Single-sample GSEA or ssGSEA calculates separate
enrichment scores for each pairing of a sample

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:09.929
and gene set.

00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:14.650
Each ssGSEA enrichment score represents the
degree to which the genes in a particular

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:19.150
gene set are coordinately up- or down-regulated
within a sample.

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:24.390
After clicking “Continue”, you are presented
with the now empty list of datasets.

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:27.950
Click the “+ Add dataset” button to add
a new dataset.

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:32.250
Now, you have to select the type of dataset
you want to load.

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:36.530
To upload your own data, select one of the
options under “Select a file from a local

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:38.010
folder”.

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:42.319
To test the tool, it is possible to quickly
load example data.

00:02:42.319 --> 00:02:48.120
Currently, ReactomeGSA provides three example
datasets, two matched datasets on melanoma

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:53.000
associated B cells and one scRNA-seq dataset
on B cells.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:58.379
Finally, it is possible to directly load datasets
from ExpressionAtlas.

00:02:58.379 --> 00:03:03.849
To do so, first navigate to ExpressionAtlas
in a separate tab or window.

00:03:03.849 --> 00:03:08.800
Once a dataset is added, you need to annotate
the experimental metadata.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:13.210
This is necessary in order to define the groups
for the differential expression analysis in

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:14.959
the next step.

00:03:14.959 --> 00:03:19.460
You can adapt the dataset’s name in the
“Dataset name” box at the top.

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:22.290
This name will be used for all results.

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:27.200
To increase the readability, we suggest to
use as short names as possible.

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:31.200
To add an annotation, click the “plus”
symbol on the right.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.849
This will add a new empty column to the table.

00:03:33.849 --> 00:03:38.879
First, add a heading to define the name of
the property, for example, “treatment”.

00:03:38.879 --> 00:03:44.730
Next, add the values for every sample that
you want to include in your comparisons.

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:48.299
Samples without any values will simply be
ignored.

00:03:48.299 --> 00:03:53.490
In the final step of adding a dataset, you
have to define which groups to compare.

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:58.040
The “comparison factor” drop-down menu
contains all parameters that were annotated

00:03:58.040 --> 00:03:59.830
in the sample table before.

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:04.439
“1st group” and “2nd group” define
which groups of samples to compare against

00:04:04.439 --> 00:04:05.980
each other.

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:10.489
Depending on which “comparison factor”
you select, the available values for the “1st

00:04:10.489 --> 00:04:13.400
group” and “2nd group” will change automatically.

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:19.739
Additionally, some gene set analysis methods
allow you to define so-called “covariates”.

00:04:19.739 --> 00:04:23.460
These are parameters that might cause a bias
in your result that you would like to correct

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:25.169
for.

00:04:25.169 --> 00:04:28.520
Simply select the relevant ones for your experiment.

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:31.910
Once you click “Continue” you will be
returned to the list of datasets where you

00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:35.410
will now see your annotated dataset in the
list.

00:04:35.410 --> 00:04:40.280
If you want, you can add any number of datasets
to a single request.

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:44.560
Once you click “Continue” from the dataset
list view, the final analysis options are

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:45.639
shown.

00:04:45.639 --> 00:04:49.940
“Create REACTOME visualizations” is always
selected.

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:55.780
If it is de-selected, the result cannot be
visualised in Reactome’s pathway browser.

00:04:55.780 --> 00:05:01.350
This option is generally only relevant to
users of the ReactomeGSA R package.

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:06.860
If you select “Create reports”, ReactomeGSA
will automatically create a Microsoft Excel

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:09.639
and PDF report of your results.

00:05:09.639 --> 00:05:13.979
Additionally, it will create a short R script
that allows you to load your data directly

00:05:13.979 --> 00:05:16.090
into an R session.

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:20.330
In case you provide your email address, you
are automatically notified as soon as the

00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:22.669
analysis is complete.

00:05:22.669 --> 00:05:27.440
The mail will contain direct links to the
generated reports and a link to the visualisation

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:29.540
in the PathwayBrowser.

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:32.970
Launch the analysis by clicking the “GO”
button.

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:37.340
If you provided an email address, you can
also close your browser.

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:41.699
The analysis will still continue on our servers
and you will be notified as soon as it is

00:05:41.699 --> 00:05:43.280
done.

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:48.440
Some analysis with many large datasets, for
example comparing five TCGA datasets in a

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:52.919
single analysis, may require up to an hour
to complete.

00:05:52.919 --> 00:05:58.380
ReactomeGSA analysis calculates up and down
regulation of genes or proteins based on the

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:00.850
submitted dataset.

00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:05.000
Results of this analysis are presented similar
to that of over-representation with a few

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:11.100
additional elements that are described below:
• Up and down regulation of genes or proteins

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:15.620
are indicated as arrow heads pointing up or
down respectively.

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:19.930
If the value is significant, there are two
arrow heads.

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:24.130
• The Analysis tab in the Details panel
contains an additional column indicating if

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:28.840
the pathway is up or down regulated, which
corresponds to the average value of all the

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:32.259
genes or proteins of that pathway.

00:06:32.259 --> 00:06:37.680
• The colour scale in the diagram panel
that indicates the expression value of genes/proteins

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:40.090
is divided into five categories.

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:45.800
o Significantly up-regulated
o Non-significantly up-regulated

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:51.160
o Not found
o Non-significantly down-regulated

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:54.420
o Significantly down-regulated

